          "buttonOnMobile": false,
          "label": "<p>In the reinforced concrete analogy of bone structure, which component plays the </p>",
          "x": 30,
          "y": 30,
          "width": 40,
          "height": 25,
          "libraryTitle": "Multiple Choice",
          "action": {
            "library": "H5P.MultiChoice 1.16",
            "params": {
              "question": "<p>In the reinforced concrete analogy of bone structure, which component plays the role of steel rebar, providing flexibility and tensile strength?</p>",
              "answers": [
                {
                  "text": "<div>Hydroxyapatite</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>Incorrect. Hydroxyapatite is the inorganic crystalline mineral that provides hardness and compressive strength — it corresponds to the concrete in the analogy, not the rebar.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>Osteoclasts</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>Incorrect. Osteoclasts are bone-resorbing cells involved in remodelling. They are not a structural component of the bone matrix itself.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>Collagen</div>",
                  "correct": true,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>Correct. Collagen is the organic protein matrix produced by osteoblasts. It constitutes about 35% of bone's dry weight and provides flexibility and tensile strength — like steel rebar preventing concrete from shattering on impact.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>Calcium carbonate</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>Incorrect. The inorganic mineral in bone is primarily hydroxyapatite (a calcium phosphate compound), not calcium carbonate. Hydroxyapatite provides compressive hardness, corresponding to concrete in the analogy.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                }
              ],
              "overallFeedback": [
                {
                  "from": 0,
                  "to": 50,
                  "feedback": "Remember the reinforced concrete analogy: collagen (organic, 35%) is the rebar that provides flexibility, while hydroxyapatite (inorganic, 65%) is the concrete that provides hardness."
                },
                {
                  "from": 51,
                  "to": 100,
                  "feedback": "Exactly right. Without collagen, bone would shatter like glass — the organic and inorganic components must work together."
                }
              ],
              "behaviour": {
                "enableRetry": true,
                "enableSolutionsButton": true,
                "enableCheckButton": true,
                "type": "auto",
