          "displayType": "poster",
          "buttonOnMobile": false,
          "label": "<p>The sagittal plane divides the body into which two portions?</p>",
          "x": 30,
          "y": 30,
          "width": 40,
          "height": 25,
          "libraryTitle": "Multiple Choice",
          "action": {
            "library": "H5P.MultiChoice 1.16",
            "params": {
              "question": "<p>The sagittal plane divides the body into which two portions?</p>",
              "answers": [
                {
                  "text": "<div>Anterior and posterior</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>Incorrect. The coronal (frontal) plane divides the body into anterior (front) and posterior (back) portions. The name 'coronal' comes from the Latin word for crown.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>Right and left</div>",
                  "correct": true,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>Correct. The sagittal plane runs vertically from front to back, dividing the body into right and left portions. When it passes exactly down the midline, it is called the midsagittal or median plane.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>Superior and inferior</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>Incorrect. The transverse (axial) plane divides the body into superior (upper) and inferior (lower) portions — this is the plane used in CT scan slices.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>Proximal and distal</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>Incorrect. Proximal and distal are directional terms for limb structures, not portions created by a body plane. The sagittal plane produces right and left portions.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                }
              ],
              "overallFeedback": [
                {
                  "from": 0,
                  "to": 50,
                  "feedback": "Remember the three planes: sagittal divides right and left, coronal divides front and back, and transverse divides upper and lower."
                },
                {
                  "from": 51,
                  "to": 100,
                  "feedback": "Well recalled. The three primary planes — sagittal (right/left), coronal (front/back), and transverse (upper/lower) — are fundamental to describing anatomical sections."
                }
              ],
              "behaviour": {
                "enableRetry": true,
                "enableSolutionsButton": true,
                "enableCheckButton": true,
                "type": "auto",
