          "buttonOnMobile": false,
          "label": "<p>A bone marrow biopsy is performed to investigate a suspected blood disorder. Fro</p>",
          "x": 30,
          "y": 30,
          "width": 40,
          "height": 25,
          "libraryTitle": "Multiple Choice",
          "action": {
            "library": "H5P.MultiChoice 1.16",
            "params": {
              "question": "<p>A bone marrow biopsy is performed to investigate a suspected blood disorder. From which anatomical site is this biopsy most commonly obtained?</p>",
              "answers": [
                {
                  "text": "<div>Medullary cavity of the femur</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>Incorrect. The medullary cavity of long bones contains yellow marrow (primarily adipocytes), which stores energy but is not the standard site for diagnostic biopsy of blood disorders.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>Body of the sternum</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>Incorrect. Although the sternum contains red marrow and sternal aspirates are occasionally performed, the standard diagnostic biopsy site is the posterior iliac crest, which provides a larger and safer area.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>Anterior iliac crest</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>Incorrect. While the iliac crest is correct, the standard approach is from the posterior iliac crest — not the anterior — as it offers a thicker area of cancellous bone with abundant red marrow.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>Posterior iliac crest</div>",
                  "correct": true,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>Correct. The posterior iliac crest is the standard site for bone marrow biopsy. It contains abundant red (haematopoietic) marrow and allows safe access for both aspiration and trephine biopsy.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                }
              ],
              "overallFeedback": [
                {
                  "from": 0,
                  "to": 50,
                  "feedback": "The posterior iliac crest is the standard bone marrow biopsy site. It is rich in red marrow, where haematopoiesis takes place, and provides safe access for both aspiration and core biopsy."
                },
                {
                  "from": 51,
                  "to": 100,
                  "feedback": "Exactly right. The posterior iliac crest is the go-to site for diagnosing leukaemia, anaemia, and other haematological disorders."
                }
              ],
              "behaviour": {
